#. type: TH
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:6
#, no-wrap
msgid "standards"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:9
msgid "standards - C and UNIX Standards"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:13
msgid ""
"The STANDARDS section that appears in many manual pages identifies various "
"standards to which the documented interface conforms.  The following list "
"briefly describes these standards."
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:13
#, no-wrap
msgid "Research Unix"
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:14
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<V7>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:24
msgid ""
"Version 7 (also known as Seventh Edition) UNIX, released by AT&T/Bell Labs "
"in 1979.  After this point, UNIX systems diverged into two main dialects: "
"BSD and System V.  Sources are E<.UR "
"https:\\://www.tuhs.org/\\:cgi-bin/\\:utree.pl?file=V7> available online "
"E<.UE .>"
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:24
#, no-wrap
msgid "BSD"
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:25
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<4.2BSD>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:38
msgid ""
"This is an implementation standard defined by the 4.2 release of the "
"I<Berkeley Software Distribution>, released by the University of California "
"at Berkeley.  This was the first Berkeley release that contained a TCP/IP "
"stack and the sockets API.  4.2BSD was released in 1983.  Sources are E<.UR "
"https:\\://www.tuhs.org/\\:cgi-bin/\\:utree.pl?file=4.2BSD> available online "
"E<.UE .>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:47
msgid ""
"Earlier major BSD releases included I<3BSD> (1980), I<4BSD> (1980), and "
"I<4.1BSD> (1981)."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:47
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<4.3BSD>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:54
msgid ""
"The successor to 4.2BSD, released in 1986.  Sources are E<.UR "
"https:\\://www.tuhs.org/\\:cgi-bin/\\:utree.pl?file=4.3BSD> available online "
"E<.UE .>"
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:54
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<4.4BSD>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:62
msgid ""
"The successor to 4.3BSD, released in 1993.  This was the last major Berkeley "
"release.  Sources are E<.UR "
"https:\\://www.tuhs.org/\\:cgi-bin/\\:utree.pl?file=4.4BSD> available online "
"E<.UE .>"
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:62
#, no-wrap
msgid "Unix/TS"
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:63
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<Unix/TS 4>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:71
msgid ""
"This was an internal release of Unix that was never released to the public "
"(in which case it would have been called System IV).  The previous major "
"AT&T release was I<System III>, released in 1981."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:71
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<System V>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:75
msgid ""
"This is an implementation standard defined by AT&T's milestone 1983 release "
"of its commercial System V (five) release."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:75
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<System V release 2 (SVr2)>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:82
msgid ""
"This was the next System V release, made in 1985.  The SVr2 was formally "
"described in the I<System V Interface Definition Issue 1> (I<SVID 1>)  "
"published in 1985."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:82
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<System V release 3 (SVr3)>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:90
msgid ""
"This was the successor to SVr2, released in 1986.  This release was formally "
"described in the E<.UR "
"https:\\://bitsavers.org/\\:pdf/\\:att/\\:unix/\\:SVID/\\:System_V_Interface_Definition_Issue_2_Volume_1_1986.pdf> "
"I<System V Interface Definition Issue 2> (I<SVID 2>)  E<.UE .>"
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:90
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<System V release 4 (SVr4)>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:102
msgid ""
"This was the successor to SVr3, released in 1989.  This version of System V "
"is described in the \"Programmer's Reference Manual: Operating System API "
"(Intel processors)\" (Prentice-Hall 1992, ISBN 0-13-951294-2)  This release "
"was formally described in the E<.UR "
"https:\\://archive.org/\\:details/\\:systemvinterface0001unse/> I<System V "
"Interface Definition Third Edition> (I<SVID 3>)  E<.UE ,> and is considered "
"the definitive System V release."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:102
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<SVID 4>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:108
msgid ""
"System V Interface Definition version 4, issued in 1995.  Available online "
"at E<.UR http:\\://www.sco.com\\:/developers\\:/devspecs/> E<.UE .>"
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:108
#, no-wrap
msgid "Standard C"
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:109
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<K&R>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:116
msgid ""
"This refers to the C language as specified in I<\\[lq]The C Programming "
"Language\\[rq]> [Brian W.\\& Kernighan and Dennis M.\\& Ritchie; Prentice "
"Hall; 1st Ed., 1978]."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:116
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<C89>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:129
msgid ""
"This was the first C language standard, ratified by ANSI (American National "
"Standards Institute) in 1989 (I<X3.159-1989>).  Sometimes this is known as "
"I<ANSI C>, but since C99 is also an ANSI standard, this term is ambiguous.  "
"It is E<.UR "
"https:\\://nvlpubs.nist.gov/\\:nistpubs/\\:Legacy/\\:FIPS/\\:fipspub160.pdf> "
"available online E<.UE .>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:134
msgid ""
"The rationale for the C89 standard is also E<.UR "
"https:\\://www.open-std.org/\\:jtc1/\\:sc22/\\:wg14/\\:www/\\:C89Rationale.pdf> "
"available online E<.UE .>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:142
msgid ""
"This standard was also ratified by ISO (International Standards "
"Organization)  and IEC (International Electrotechnical Commision)  in 1990 "
"(I<ISO/IEC\\~9899:1990>), and is thus occasionally referred to as "
"I<ISO\\~C90>."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:142
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<C94>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:149
msgid ""
"E<.UR "
"https:\\://www.open-std.org/\\:jtc1/\\:sc22/\\:wg14/\\:www/\\:docs/\\:tc1.htm> "
"Technical Corrigendum 1 E<.UE> of C90 was ratified by ISO/IEC in 1994 "
"(I<ISO/IEC\\~9899:1990/Cor1:1994>)."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:149
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<C95>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:157
msgid ""
"This amendment of the C language standard was ratified by ISO/IEC in 1995 "
"(I<ISO/IEC\\~9899:1990/Amd1:1995>).  A draft is E<.UR "
"https:\\://www.open-std.org/\\:jtc1/\\:sc22/\\:wg14/\\:www/\\:docs/\\:n412.pdf> "
"available online E<.UE .>"
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:157
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<C96>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:168
msgid ""
"E<.UR "
"https:\\://www.open-std.org/\\:jtc1/\\:sc22/\\:wg14/\\:www/\\:docs/\\:tc2.htm> "
"Technical Corrigendum 2 E<.UE> of C90 was ratified by ISO/IEC in 1996 "
"(I<ISO/IEC\\~9899:1990/Cor2:1996>).  A draft is E<.UR "
"https:\\://www.open-std.org/\\:jtc1/\\:sc22/\\:wg14/\\:www/\\:docs/\\:n457.pdf> "
"available online E<.UE .>"
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:168
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<C99>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:172
msgid ""
"This revision of the C language standard was ratified by ISO/IEC in 1999 "
"(I<ISO/IEC\\~9899:1999>)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:178
msgid ""
"E<.UR "
"https:\\://www.open-std.org/\\:jtc1/\\:sc22/\\:wg14/\\:www/\\:docs/\\:9899tc1/\\:n32071.PDF> "
"Technical Corrigendum 1 E<.UE> of C99 was ratified by ISO/IEC in 2001 "
"(I<ISO/IEC\\~9899:1999/Cor1:2001>)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:184
msgid ""
"E<.UR "
"https:\\://www.open-std.org/\\:jtc1/\\:sc22/\\:wg14/\\:www/\\:docs/\\:9899-1999_cor_2-2004.pdf> "
"Technical Corrigendum 2 E<.UE> of C99 was ratified by ISO/IEC in 2004 "
"(I<ISO/IEC\\~9899:1999/Cor2:2004>)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:194
msgid ""
"E<.UR "
"https:\\://www.open-std.org/\\:JTC1/\\:SC22/\\:WG14/\\:www/\\:docs/\\:n1235.pdf> "
"Technical Corrigendum 3 E<.UE> of C99 was ratified by ISO/IEC in 2007 "
"(I<ISO/IEC\\~9899:1999/Cor3:2007>).  A draft is E<.UR "
"https:\\://www.open-std.org/\\:jtc1/\\:sc22/\\:wg14/\\:www/\\:docs/\\:n1256.pdf> "
"available online E<.UE .>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:199
msgid ""
"The rationale for the C99 standard is also E<.UR "
"https:\\://www.open-std.org/\\:jtc1/\\:sc22/\\:wg14/\\:www/\\:docs/\\:C99RationaleV5.10.pdf> "
"available online E<.UE .>"
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:199
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<C11>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:207
msgid ""
"This revision of the C language standard was ratified by ISO/IEC in 2011 "
"(I<ISO/IEC\\~9899:2011>).  A draft is E<.UR "
"https:\\://www.open-std.org/\\:jtc1/\\:sc22/\\:wg14/\\:www/\\:docs/\\:n1570.pdf> "
"available online E<.UE .>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:213
msgid ""
"E<.UR "
"https:\\://www.open-std.org/\\:jtc1/\\:sc22/\\:wg14/\\:www/\\:docs/\\:n1606.pdf> "
"Technical Corrigendum 1 E<.UE> of C11 was ratified by ISO/IEC in 2012 "
"(I<ISO/IEC\\~9899:2011/Cor1:2012>)."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:213
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<C17>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:221
msgid ""
"This revision of the C language standard was ratified by ISO/IEC in 2018 "
"(I<ISO/IEC\\~9899:2018>).  A draft is E<.UR "
"https:\\://web.archive.org/\\:web/\\:20181230041359if_/\\:http:\\://www.open-std.org/\\:jtc1/\\:sc22/\\:wg14/\\:www/\\:abq/\\:c17_updated_proposed_fdis.pdf> "
"available online E<.UE .>"
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:221
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<C23>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:229
msgid ""
"This revision of the C language standard was ratified by ISO/IEC in 2024 "
"(I<ISO/IEC\\~9899:2024>).  A draft is E<.UR "
"https:\\://www.open-std.org/\\:jtc1/\\:sc22/\\:wg14/\\:www/\\:docs/\\:n3220.pdf> "
"available online E<.UE .>"
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:229
#, no-wrap
msgid "POSIX and SUS"
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:230
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<POSIX.1-1988>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:240
msgid ""
"This was the first POSIX standard, ratified by IEEE in 1998 "
"(I<IEEE\\~Std\\~1003.1-1988>).  The term \"POSIX\" was coined by Richard "
"Stallman.  It is E<.UR "
"https:\\://nvlpubs.nist.gov/\\:nistpubs/\\:Legacy/\\:FIPS/\\:fipspub151-1.pdf> "
"available online E<.UE .>"
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:240
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<POSIX.1-1990>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:252
msgid ""
"Part 1, \"Portable Operating System Interface for Computing Environments\".  "
"Ratified by IEEE in 1990, (I<IEEE\\~Std\\~1003.1-1990>), and ratified by "
"ISO/IEC in 1990 (I<ISO/IEC\\~9945-1:1990>).  It is E<.UR "
"https:\\://nvlpubs.nist.gov/\\:nistpubs/\\:Legacy/\\:FIPS/\\:fipspub151-2.pdf> "
"available online E<.UE .>"
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:252
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<POSIX.2>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:260
msgid ""
"Part 2, which describes commands and utilities.  Ratified by IEEE in 1992 "
"(I<IEEE\\~Std\\~1003.2-1992>), and ratified by ISO/IEC in 1993 "
"(I<ISO/IEC\\~9945-2:1993>)."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:260
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<POSIX.1b> (formerly known as \\f[I]POSIX.4\\fR)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:266
msgid ""
"Part 1b, which describes real-time facilities for portable operating "
"systems.  Ratified by IEEE in 1993 (I<IEEE\\~Std\\~1003.1b-1993>)."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:266
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<POSIX.1c> (formerly known as \\f[I]POSIX.4a\\fR)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:272
msgid ""
"Part 1c, which describes the POSIX threads interfaces.  Ratified by IEEE in "
"1995 (I<IEEE\\~Std\\~1003.1c-1995>)."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:272
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<POSIX.1d>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:278
msgid ""
"Part 1d, which describes additional real-time extensions.  Ratified by IEEE "
"in 1999 (I<IEEE\\~Std\\~1003.1d-1999>)."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:278
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<POSIX.1g>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:284
msgid ""
"Part 1g, which describes networking APIs (including sockets).  Ratified by "
"IEEE in 2000 (I<IEEE\\~Std\\~1003.1g-2000>)."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:284
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<POSIX.1j>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:290
msgid ""
"Part 1j, which describes advanced real-time extensions.  Ratified by IEEE in "
"2000 (I<IEEE\\~Std\\~1003.1j-2000>)."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:290
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<POSIX.1-1996>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:295
msgid ""
"A revision of POSIX.1 which incorporated POSIX.1b and POSIX.1c.  Ratified by "
"ISO/IEC in 1996 (I<ISO/IEC\\~9945-1:1996>)."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:295
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<XPG3>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:303
msgid ""
"Released in 1989, this was the first release of the X/Open Portability Guide "
"to be based on a POSIX standard (POSIX.1-1988).  It is also known as "
"I<Issue\\~3>.  This multivolume guide was developed by the X/Open Group, a "
"multivendor consortium."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:303
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<XPG4>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:309
msgid ""
"A revision of the X/Open Portability Guide, released in 1992.  It is also "
"known as I<Issue\\~4>.  This revision incorporated POSIX.2."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:309
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<XPG4v2>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:316
msgid ""
"A 1994 revision of XPG4.  This is also referred to as I<Spec\\~1170>, where "
"1170 referred to the number of interfaces defined by this standard."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:316
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<SUS\\~(SUSv1)>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:324
msgid ""
"Single UNIX Specification.  This was a repackaging of XPG4v2 and other "
"X/Open standards (X/Open Curses Issue 4 version 2, X/Open Networking Service "
"(XNS) Issue 4).  Systems conforming to this standard can be branded "
"I<UNIX\\~95>."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:324
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<SUSv2>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:335
msgid ""
"Single UNIX Specification version 2.  Sometimes also referred to as "
"I<Issue\\~5>.  This standard appeared in 1997.  Systems conforming to this "
"standard can be branded I<UNIX\\~98>.  See also E<.UR "
"http:\\://www.unix.org\\:/version2/> E<.UE .)>"
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:335
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<POSIX.1-2001>"
msgstr ""

#. type: TQ
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:337
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<SUSv3>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:347
msgid ""
"This was a 2001 revision and consolidation of the POSIX.1, POSIX.2, and SUS "
"standards into a single document, conducted under the auspices of the Austin "
"Group E<.UR http:\\://www.opengroup.org\\:/austin/> E<.UE .> The standard is "
"available online at E<.UR http:\\://www.unix.org\\:/version3/> E<.UE .>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:357
msgid ""
"The standard defines two levels of conformance: I<POSIX conformance>, which "
"is a baseline set of interfaces required of a conforming system; and I<XSI "
"Conformance>, which additionally mandates a set of interfaces (the \"XSI "
"extension\") which are only optional for POSIX conformance.  XSI-conformant "
"systems can be branded I<UNIX\\~03>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:359
msgid "The POSIX.1-2001 document is broken into four parts:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:362
msgid "B<XBD>: Definitions, terms, and concepts, header file specifications."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:366
msgid ""
"B<XSH>: Specifications of functions (i.e., system calls and library "
"functions in actual implementations)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:370
msgid ""
"B<XCU>: Specifications of commands and utilities (i.e., the area formerly "
"described by POSIX.2)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:373
msgid "B<XRAT>: Informative text on the other parts of the standard."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:377
msgid ""
"POSIX.1-2001 is aligned with C99, so that all of the library functions "
"standardized in C99 are also standardized in POSIX.1-2001."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:382
msgid ""
"The Single UNIX Specification version 3 (SUSv3) comprises the Base "
"Specifications containing XBD, XSH, XCU, and XRAT as above, plus X/Open "
"Curses Issue 4 version 2 as an extra volume that is not in POSIX.1-2001."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:382
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<POSIX.1-2001 2002 edition>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:388
msgid ""
"Technical Corrigendum 1 of POSIX.1-2001 was ratified by IEEE in 2002 "
"(I<IEEE\\~Std\\~1003.1-2001/Cor1-2002>)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:391
msgid "This is informally known as I<POSIX.1-2002>."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:391
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<POSIX.1-2001 2004 edition>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:401
msgid ""
"Technical Corrigendum 2 of POSIX.1-2001 was ratified by IEEE in 2004 "
"(I<IEEE\\~Std\\~1003.1-2001/Cor2-2004>).  It is E<.UR "
"https:\\://pubs.opengroup.org/\\:onlinepubs/\\:009695399/> available online "
"E<.UE .>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:404
msgid "This is informally known as I<POSIX.1-2004>."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:404
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<POSIX.1-2008>"
msgstr ""

#. type: TQ
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:406
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<SUSv4>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:417
msgid ""
"Work on the next revision of POSIX.1/SUS was completed and ratified in "
"2008.  The standard is available online at E<.UR "
"http:\\://www.unix.org\\:/version4/> E<.UE ,> and also E<.UR "
"https:\\://pubs.opengroup.org/\\:onlinepubs/\\:9699919799.2008edition/> here "
"E<.UE .>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:426
msgid ""
"The changes in this revision are not as large as those that occurred for "
"POSIX.1-2001/SUSv3, but a number of new interfaces are added and various "
"details of existing specifications are modified.  Many of the interfaces "
"that were optional in POSIX.1-2001 become mandatory in the 2008 revision of "
"the standard.  A few interfaces that are present in POSIX.1-2001 are marked "
"as obsolete in POSIX.1-2008, or removed from the standard altogether."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:432
msgid ""
"The revised standard is structured in the same way as its predecessor.  The "
"Single UNIX Specification version 4 (SUSv4) comprises the Base "
"Specifications containing XBD, XSH, XCU, and XRAT, plus X/Open Curses Issue "
"7 as an extra volume that is not in POSIX.1-2008."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:439
msgid ""
"Again there are two levels of conformance: the baseline I<POSIX "
"Conformance>, and I<XSI Conformance>, which mandates an additional set of "
"interfaces beyond those in the base specification."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:443
msgid ""
"In general, where the STANDARDS section of a manual page lists POSIX.1-2001, "
"it can be assumed that the interface also conforms to POSIX.1-2008, unless "
"otherwise noted."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:447
msgid ""
"Further information can be found on the Austin Group web site, E<.UR "
"http:\\://www.opengroup.org\\:/austin/> E<.UE .>"
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:447
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<POSIX.1-2008 2013 edition>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:457
msgid ""
"Technical Corrigendum 1 of POSIX.1-2008 was ratified by IEEE in 2013 "
"(I<IEEE\\~Std\\~1003.1-2008/Cor1-2013>).  It is E<.UR "
"https:\\://pubs.opengroup.org/\\:onlinepubs/\\:9699919799.2013edition/> "
"available online E<.UE .>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:460
msgid "This is informally known as I<POSIX.1-2013>."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:460
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<POSIX.1-2008 2016 edition>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:470
msgid ""
"Technical Corrigendum 2 of POSIX.1-2008 was ratified by IEEE in 2016 "
"(I<IEEE\\~Std\\~1003.1-2008/Cor2-2016>).  It is E<.UR "
"https:\\://pubs.opengroup.org/\\:onlinepubs/\\:9699919799.2016edition/> "
"available online E<.UE .>"
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:470
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<SUSv4 2016 edition>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:474
msgid ""
"This is equivalent to POSIX.1-2008 2016 edition, with the addition of the "
"XCurses specification."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:474
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<POSIX.1-2017>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:484
msgid ""
"This revision of POSIX is technically identical to POSIX.1-2008 2016 "
"edition.  Ratified by IEEE in 2017 (I<IEEE\\~Std\\~1003.1-2017>).  It is "
"E<.UR https:\\://pubs.opengroup.org/\\:onlinepubs/\\:9699919799/> available "
"online E<.UE .>"
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:484
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<SUSv4 2018 edition>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:488
msgid ""
"This is equivalent to POSIX.1-2017, with the addition of the XCurses "
"specification."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:488
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<POSIX.1-2024>"
msgstr ""

#. type: TQ
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:490
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<SUSv5>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:499
msgid ""
"Work on the next revision of POSIX.1/SUS was completed and ratified by IEEE "
"in 2024 (I<IEEE\\~Std\\~1003.1-2024>).  It is E<.UR "
"https:\\://pubs.opengroup.org/\\:onlinepubs/\\:9799919799/> available online "
"E<.UE .>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:503
msgid ""
"POSIX.1-2024 is aligned with C17, so that all of the library functions "
"standardized in C17 are also standardized in POSIX.1-2024."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:508
msgid ""
"Further information can be found on the E<.UR "
"http:\\://www.opengroup.org/\\:austin/> Austin Group web site E<.UE .>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:514
msgid ""
"The interfaces documented in POSIX.1/SUS are available as manual pages under "
"sections 0p (header files), 1p (commands), and 3p (functions); thus one can "
"write I<man\\~3p\\~open>."
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:514
#, no-wrap
msgid "Other standards"
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:515
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<LFS>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/standards.7:523
msgid ""
"The Large File Summit specification, completed in 1996.  This specification "
"defined mechanisms that allowed 32-bit systems to support the use of large "
"files (i.e., 64-bit file offsets).  See E<.UR "
"https:\\://www.opengroup.org\\:/platform\\:/lfs.html> E<.UE .>"
