#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/landlock.7:488
msgid ""
"Users should use the Landlock ABI version rather than the kernel version to "
"determine which features are available.  The mainline kernel versions listed "
"here are only included for orientation.  Kernels from other sources may "
"contain backported features, and their version numbers may not match."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/landlock.7:494
msgid ""
"To query the running kernel's Landlock ABI version, programs may pass the "
"B<LANDLOCK_CREATE_RULESET_VERSION> flag to B<landlock_create_ruleset>(2)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/landlock.7:503
msgid ""
"When building fallback mechanisms for compatibility with older kernels, "
"users are advised to consider the special semantics of the "
"B<LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_REFER> access right: In ABI v1, linking and moving of "
"files between different directories is always forbidden, so programs relying "
"on such operations are only compatible with Landlock ABI v2 and higher."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/landlock.7:521
msgid ""
"Landlock is enabled by B<CONFIG_SECURITY_LANDLOCK>.  The "
"I<lsm=lsm1,...,lsmN> command line parameter controls the sequence of the "
"initialization of Linux Security Modules.  It must contain the string "
"I<landlock> to enable Landlock.  If the command line parameter is not "
"specified, the initialization falls back to the value of the deprecated "
"I<security=> command line parameter and further to the value of "
"B<CONFIG_LSM>.  We can check that Landlock is enabled by looking for "
"I<landlock: Up and running.> in kernel logs."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/landlock.7:534
msgid ""
"It is currently not possible to restrict some file-related actions "
"accessible through these system call families: B<chdir>(2), B<stat>(2), "
"B<flock>(2), B<chmod>(2), B<chown>(2), B<setxattr>(2), B<utime>(2), "
"B<fcntl>(2), B<access>(2).  Future Landlock evolutions will enable to "
"restrict them."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/landlock.7:536
msgid "We first need to create the ruleset that will contain our rules."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/landlock.7:544
msgid ""
"For this example, the ruleset will contain rules that only allow read "
"actions, but write actions will be denied.  The ruleset then needs to handle "
"both of these kinds of actions.  See the B<DESCRIPTION> section for the "
"description of filesystem actions."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/landlock.7:567
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct landlock_ruleset_attr attr = {0};\n"
"int ruleset_fd;\n"
"\\&\n"
"attr.handled_access_fs =\n"
"        LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_EXECUTE |\n"
"        LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_WRITE_FILE |\n"
"        LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_READ_FILE |\n"
"        LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_READ_DIR |\n"
"        LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_REMOVE_DIR |\n"
"        LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_REMOVE_FILE |\n"
"        LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_MAKE_CHAR |\n"
"        LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_MAKE_DIR |\n"
"        LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_MAKE_REG |\n"
"        LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_MAKE_SOCK |\n"
"        LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_MAKE_FIFO |\n"
"        LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_MAKE_BLOCK |\n"
"        LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_MAKE_SYM |\n"
"        LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_REFER |\n"
"        LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_TRUNCATE |\n"
"        LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_IOCTL_DEV;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/landlock.7:573
msgid ""
"To be compatible with older Linux versions, we detect the available Landlock "
"ABI version, and only use the available subset of access rights:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/landlock.7:602
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"/*\n"
" * Table of available file system access rights by ABI version,\n"
" * numbers hardcoded to keep the example short.\n"
" */\n"
"__u64 landlock_fs_access_rights[] = {\n"
"    (LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_MAKE_SYM  E<lt>E<lt> 1) - 1,  /* v1                  "
"*/\n"
"    (LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_REFER     E<lt>E<lt> 1) - 1,  /* v2: add \"refer\"     "
"*/\n"
"    (LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_TRUNCATE  E<lt>E<lt> 1) - 1,  /* v3: add "
"\"truncate\"  */\n"
"    (LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_TRUNCATE  E<lt>E<lt> 1) - 1,  /* v4: TCP support     "
"*/\n"
"    (LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_IOCTL_DEV E<lt>E<lt> 1) - 1,  /* v5: add "
"\"ioctl_dev\" */\n"
"};\n"
"\\&\n"
"int abi = landlock_create_ruleset(NULL, 0,\n"
"                                  LANDLOCK_CREATE_RULESET_VERSION);\n"
"if (abi == -1) {\n"
"    /*\n"
"     * Kernel too old, not compiled with Landlock,\n"
"     * or Landlock was not enabled at boot time.\n"
"     */\n"
"    perror(\"Unable to use Landlock\");\n"
"    return;  /* Graceful fallback: Do nothing.  */\n"
"}\n"
"abi = MIN(abi, 3);\n"
"\\&\n"
"/* Only use the available rights in the ruleset.  */\n"
"attr.handled_access_fs &= landlock_fs_access_rights[abi - 1];\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/landlock.7:608
msgid ""
"The available access rights for each ABI version are listed in the "
"B<VERSIONS> section."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/landlock.7:619
msgid ""
"If our program needed to create hard links or rename files between different "
"directories (B<LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_REFER>), we would require the following "
"change to the backwards compatibility logic: Directory reparenting is not "
"possible in a process restricted with Landlock ABI version 1.  Therefore, if "
"the program needed to do file reparenting, and if only Landlock ABI version "
"1 was available, we could not restrict the process."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/landlock.7:625
msgid ""
"Now that the ruleset attributes are determined, we create the Landlock "
"ruleset and acquire a file descriptor as a handle to it, using "
"B<landlock_create_ruleset>(2):"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/landlock.7:633
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"ruleset_fd = landlock_create_ruleset(&attr, sizeof(attr), 0);\n"
"if (ruleset_fd == -1) {\n"
"    perror(\"Failed to create a ruleset\");\n"
"    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n"
"}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/landlock.7:641
msgid ""
"We can now add a new rule to the ruleset through the ruleset's file "
"descriptor.  The requested access rights must be a subset of the access "
"rights which were specified in I<attr.handled_access_fs> at ruleset creation "
"time."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/landlock.7:652
msgid ""
"In this example, the rule will only allow reading the file hierarchy "
"I</usr>.  Without another rule, write actions would then be denied by the "
"ruleset.  To add I</usr> to the ruleset, we open it with the I<O_PATH> flag "
"and fill the I<struct landlock_path_beneath_attr> with this file descriptor."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/landlock.7:677
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct landlock_path_beneath_attr path_beneath = {0};\n"
"int err;\n"
"\\&\n"
"path_beneath.allowed_access =\n"
"        LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_EXECUTE |\n"
"        LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_READ_FILE |\n"
"        LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_READ_DIR;\n"
"\\&\n"
"path_beneath.parent_fd = open(\"/usr\", O_PATH | O_CLOEXEC);\n"
"if (path_beneath.parent_fd == -1) {\n"
"    perror(\"Failed to open file\");\n"
"    close(ruleset_fd);\n"
"    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n"
"}\n"
"err = landlock_add_rule(ruleset_fd, LANDLOCK_RULE_PATH_BENEATH,\n"
"                        &path_beneath, 0);\n"
"close(path_beneath.parent_fd);\n"
"if (err) {\n"
"    perror(\"Failed to update ruleset\");\n"
"    close(ruleset_fd);\n"
"    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n"
"}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/landlock.7:686
msgid ""
"We now have a ruleset with one rule allowing read access to I</usr> while "
"denying all other handled accesses for the filesystem.  The next step is to "
"restrict the current thread from gaining more privileges (e.g., thanks to a "
"set-user-ID binary)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/landlock.7:694
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"if (prctl(PR_SET_NO_NEW_PRIVS, 1, 0, 0, 0)) {\n"
"    perror(\"Failed to restrict privileges\");\n"
"    close(ruleset_fd);\n"
"    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n"
"}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/landlock.7:698
msgid "The current thread is now ready to sandbox itself with the ruleset."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/landlock.7:707
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"if (landlock_restrict_self(ruleset_fd, 0)) {\n"
"    perror(\"Failed to enforce ruleset\");\n"
"    close(ruleset_fd);\n"
"    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n"
"}\n"
"close(ruleset_fd);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/landlock.7:718
msgid ""
"If the B<landlock_restrict_self>(2)  system call succeeds, the current "
"thread is now restricted and this policy will be enforced on all its "
"subsequently created children as well.  Once a thread is landlocked, there "
"is no way to remove its security policy; only adding more restrictions is "
"allowed.  These threads are now in a new Landlock domain, merge of their "
"parent one (if any) with the new ruleset."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/landlock.7:722
msgid ""
"Full working code can be found in E<.UR "
"https:\\://git.kernel.org/\\:pub/\\:scm/\\:linux/\\:kernel/\\:git/\\:stable/\\:linux.git/\\:tree/\\:samples/\\:landlock/\\:sandboxer.c> "
"E<.UE>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/landlock.7:726
msgid ""
"B<landlock_create_ruleset>(2), B<landlock_add_rule>(2), "
"B<landlock_restrict_self>(2)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/landlock.7:728
msgid "E<.UR https:\\://landlock.io/> E<.UE>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/pathname.7:12
msgid "pathname, filename - how pathnames are encoded and interpreted"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/pathname.7:17
msgid ""
"Some system calls allow you to pass a pathname as a parameter.  When writing "
"code that deals with pathnames, there are kernel-space requirements that you "
"must comply with, and user-space requirements that you should comply with."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/pathname.7:22
msgid ""
"The kernel stores pathnames as C strings, that is, sequences of non-null "
"bytes terminated by a null byte.  There are a few general rules that apply "
"to all pathnames:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/pathname.7:24
msgid "The last byte in the sequence needs to be a null byte."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/pathname.7:26
msgid "Any other bytes in the sequence need to be non-null bytes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/pathname.7:31
msgid ""
"A 0x2F byte (I<\\[aq]/\\[aq]>)  is always interpreted as a directory "
"separator, and cannot be part of a filename."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/pathname.7:43
msgid ""
"A pathname can be at most B<PATH_MAX> bytes long (see B<limits.h>(0p)).  A "
"pathname that's longer than B<PATH_MAX> bytes can be split into multiple "
"smaller pathnames and opened piecewise using B<openat>(2)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/pathname.7:60
msgid ""
"A filename can be at most a certain number of bytes long.  The number is "
"filesystem-specific (see B<_PC_NAME_MAX> in B<fpathconf>(3)).  For maximum "
"portability, programs should be able to handle filenames that are as long as "
"the relevant filesystems will allow.  For maximum portability, programs and "
"users should limit the length of their own pathnames to B<NAME_MAX> bytes "
"(see B<limits.h>(0p))."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/pathname.7:64
msgid ""
"Some filesystems or APIs may apply further restrictions, such as requiring "
"shorter filenames, or restricting the allowed bytes in a filename."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/pathname.7:72
msgid ""
"For maximum interoperability, programs and users should also limit the "
"characters that they use for their own pathnames to characters in the POSIX "
"E<.UR "
"https:\\://pubs.opengroup.org/\\:onlinepubs/\\:9799919799/\\:basedefs/\\:V1_chap03.html#tag_03_265> "
"Portable Filename Character Set E<.UE .>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/pathname.7:77
msgid ""
"POSIX.1-2024 encourages implementations to disallow creation of filenames "
"containing new-line characters.  Linux doesn't follow this, and allows using "
"new-line characters."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/pathname.7:82
msgid ""
"B<limits.h>(0p), B<open>(2), B<fpathconf>(3), B<path_resolution>(7), "
"B<mount>(8)"
msgstr ""

#. type: TH
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:5
#, no-wrap
msgid "string_copying"
msgstr ""

#.  ----- SYNOPSIS :: -------------------------------------------------/
#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:18
msgid ""
"stpcpy, strcpy, strcat, stpecpy, strtcpy, strlcpy, strlcat, stpncpy, "
"strncpy, strncat - copying strings and character sequences"
msgstr ""

#.  ----- SYNOPSIS :: (Null-terminated) strings -----------------------/
#. type: SS
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:20
#, no-wrap
msgid "Strings"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:24
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"// Chain-copy a string.\n"
"B<char *stpcpy(char *restrict >I<dst>B<, const char *restrict >I<src>B<);>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:28
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"// Copy/catenate a string.\n"
"B<char *strcpy(char *restrict >I<dst>B<, const char *restrict >I<src>B<);>\n"
"B<char *strcat(char *restrict >I<dst>B<, const char *restrict >I<src>B<);>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:31
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"// Chain-copy a string with truncation.\n"
"B<char *stpecpy(char *>I<dst>B<, char >I<end>B<[0], const char *restrict "
">I<src>B<);>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:42
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"// Copy/catenate a string with truncation.\n"
"B<ssize_t strtcpy(>size_t dsize;\n"
"B<               char >I<dst>B<[restrict >I<dsize>B<], const char *restrict "
">I<src>B<,>\n"
"B<               size_t >I<dsize>B<);>\n"
"B<size_t strlcpy(>size_t dsize;\n"
"B<               char >I<dst>B<[restrict >I<dsize>B<], const char *restrict "
">I<src>B<,>\n"
"B<               size_t >I<dsize>B<);>\n"
"B<size_t strlcat(>size_t dsize;\n"
"B<               char >I<dst>B<[restrict >I<dsize>B<], const char *restrict "
">I<src>B<,>\n"
"B<               size_t >I<dsize>B<);>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:44 build/C/man7/string_copying.7:244
#, no-wrap
msgid "Null-padded character sequences"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:54
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"// Fill a fixed-size buffer with characters from a string\n"
"// and pad with null bytes.\n"
"B<char *strncpy(>size_t dsize;\n"
"B<              char >I<dst>B<[restrict >I<dsize>B<], const char *restrict "
">I<src>B<,>\n"
"B<               size_t >I<dsize>B<);>\n"
"B<char *stpncpy(>size_t dsize;\n"
"B<              char >I<dst>B<[restrict >I<dsize>B<], const char *restrict "
">I<src>B<,>\n"
"B<               size_t >I<dsize>B<);>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:57
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"// Chain-copy a null-padded character sequence into a character sequence.\n"
"I<mempcpy(dst, src, strnlen(src, countof(src)));>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:60
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"// Chain-copy a null-padded character sequence into a string.\n"
"I<stpcpy(mempcpy(dst, src, strnlen(src, countof(src))), \\[dq]\\[dq]);>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:65
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"// Catenate a null-padded character sequence into a string.\n"
"B<char *strncat(>size_t ssize;\n"
"B<               char *restrict >I<dst>B<, const char >I<src>B<[restrict "
">I<ssize>B<],>\n"
"B<               size_t >I<ssize>B<);>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:69
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"// Duplicate a null-padded character sequence into a string.\n"
"B<char *strndup(>size_t ssize;\n"
"B<               const char >I<src>B<[>I<ssize>B<], size_t >I<ssize>B<);>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:71 build/C/man7/string_copying.7:270
#, no-wrap
msgid "Length-bounded character sequences"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:77
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"// Chain-copy a length-bounded character sequence.\n"
"B<void *mempcpy(>size_t len;\n"
"B<               void >I<dst>B<[restrict >I<len>B<], const void "
">I<src>B<[restrict >I<len>B<],>\n"
"B<               size_t >I<len>B<);>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:80
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"// Chain-copy a length-bounded character sequence into a string.\n"
"I<stpcpy(mempcpy(dst, src, len), \\[dq]\\[dq]);>\n"
msgstr ""

#.  ----- DESCRIPTION :: Terms (and abbreviations) :: -----------------/
#. type: SS
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:83
#, no-wrap
msgid "Terms (and abbreviations)"
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:85
#, no-wrap
msgid "I<string >(I<str>)"
msgstr ""

#.  ----- DESCRIPTION :: Terms (and abbreviations) :: null-padded character seq
#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:89
msgid ""
"is a sequence of zero or more non-null characters followed by a null "
"character."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:89
#, no-wrap
msgid "I<character sequence>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:95
msgid ""
"is a sequence of zero or more non-null characters.  A program should never "
"use a character sequence where a string is required.  However, with "
"appropriate care, a string can be used in the place of a character sequence."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:96
#, no-wrap
msgid "I<null-padded character sequence>"
msgstr ""

#.  ----- DESCRIPTION :: Terms (and abbreviations) :: length-bounded character sequence
#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:107
msgid ""
"Character sequences can be contained in fixed-size buffers, which contain "
"padding null bytes after the character sequence, to fill the rest of the "
"buffer without affecting the character sequence; however, those padding null "
"bytes are not part of the character sequence.  Don't confuse null-padded "
"with null-terminated: null-padded means 0 or more padding null bytes, while "
"null-terminated means exactly 1 terminating null character."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:107
#, no-wrap
msgid "I<length-bounded character sequence>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:112
msgid ""
"Character sequence delimited by its length.  It may be a slice of a larger "
"character sequence, or even of a string."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:114
#, no-wrap
msgid "I<length >(I<len>)"
msgstr ""

#.  ----- DESCRIPTION :: Terms (and abbreviations) :: size ------------/
#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:122
msgid ""
"is the number of non-null characters in a string or character sequence.  It "
"is the return value of I<strlen(str)> and of I<strnlen(buf, size)>."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:122
#, no-wrap
msgid "I<size>"
msgstr ""

#.  ----- DESCRIPTION :: Terms (and abbreviations) :: end -------------/
#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:127
msgid ""
"refers to the entire buffer where the string or character sequence is "
"contained."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:127
#, no-wrap
msgid "I<end>"
msgstr ""

#.  ----- DESCRIPTION :: Terms (and abbreviations) :: copy ------------/
#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:136
msgid ""
"is the name of a pointer to one past the last element of a buffer.  It is "
"equivalent to I<&str[size]>.  It is used as a sentinel value, to be able to "
"truncate strings or character sequences instead of overrunning the "
"containing buffer."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:136
#, no-wrap
msgid "I<copy>"
msgstr ""

#.  ----- DESCRIPTION :: Terms (and abbreviations) :: catenate --------/
#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:142
msgid ""
"This term is used when the writing starts at the first element pointed to by "
"I<dst>."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:142
#, no-wrap
msgid "I<catenate>"
msgstr ""

#.  ----- DESCRIPTION :: Terms (and abbreviations) :: chain -----------/
#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:149
msgid ""
"This term is used when a function first finds the terminating null character "
"in I<dst>, and then starts writing at that position."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:149
#, no-wrap
msgid "I<chain>"
msgstr ""

#.  ----- DESCRIPTION :: Terms (and abbreviations) :: duplicate -------/
#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:163
msgid ""
"This term is used when it's the programmer who provides a pointer to the "
"terminating null character in the string I<dst> (or one after the last "
"character in a character sequence), and the function starts writing at that "
"location.  The function returns a pointer to the new location of the "
"terminating null character (or one after the last character in a character "
"sequence)  after the call, so that the programmer can use it to chain such "
"calls."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:163
#, no-wrap
msgid "I<duplicate>"
msgstr ""

#.  ----- DESCRIPTION :: Copy, catenate, and chain-copy ---------------/
#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:168
msgid "Allocate a new buffer where a copy is placed."
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:168
#, no-wrap
msgid "Copy, catenate, and chain-copy"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:179
msgid ""
"Originally, there was a distinction between functions that copy and those "
"that catenate.  However, newer functions that copy while allowing chaining "
"cover both use cases with a single API.  They are also algorithmically "
"faster, since they don't need to search for the terminating null character "
"of the existing string.  However, functions that catenate have a much "
"simpler use, so if performance is not important, it can make sense to use "
"them for improving readability."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:189
msgid ""
"The pointer returned by functions that allow chaining is a byproduct of the "
"copy operation, so it has no performance costs.  Functions that return such "
"a pointer, and thus can be chained, have names of the form *B<stp>*(), since "
"it's common to name the pointer just I<p>."
msgstr ""

#.  ----- DESCRIPTION :: Truncate or not? -----------------------------/
#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:196
msgid ""
"Chain-copying functions that truncate should accept a pointer to the end of "
"the destination buffer, and have names of the form *B<stpe>*().  This allows "
"not having to recalculate the remaining size after each call."
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:196
#, no-wrap
msgid "Truncate or not?"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:200
msgid ""
"The first thing to note is that programmers should be careful with buffers, "
"so they always have the correct size, and truncation is not necessary."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:207
msgid ""
"In most cases, truncation is not desired, and it is simpler to just do the "
"copy.  Simpler code is safer code.  Programming against programming mistakes "
"by adding more code just adds more points where mistakes can be made."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:217
msgid ""
"Nowadays, compilers can detect most programmer errors with features like "
"compiler warnings, static analyzers, and B<\\%_FORTIFY_SOURCE> (see "
"B<ftm>(7)).  Keeping the code simple helps these overflow-detection features "
"be more precise."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:221
msgid ""
"When validating user input, code should normally not truncate, but instead "
"fail and prevent the copy at all."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:225
msgid "In some cases, however, it makes sense to truncate."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:227
msgid "Functions that truncate:"
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:229 build/C/man7/string_copying.7:329
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:389 build/C/man7/string_copying.7:495
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:544 build/C/man7/string_copying.7:638
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<stpecpy>()"
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:231 build/C/man7/string_copying.7:331
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:403 build/C/man7/string_copying.7:510
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:546 build/C/man7/string_copying.7:654
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<strtcpy>()"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:237
msgid ""
"B<strlcpy>(3bsd)  and B<strlcat>(3bsd)  are similar, but have important "
"performance problems; see BUGS."
msgstr ""

#.  ----- DESCRIPTION :: Null-padded character sequences --------------/
#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:244
msgid ""
"B<stpncpy>(3)  and B<strncpy>(3)  also truncate, but they don't write "
"strings, but rather null-padded character sequences."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:254
msgid ""
"For historic reasons, some standard APIs and file formats, such as "
"B<utmpx>(5)  and B<tar>(1), use null-padded character sequences in "
"fixed-size buffers.  To interface with them, specialized functions need to "
"be used."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:259
msgid ""
"To copy bytes from strings into these buffers, use B<strncpy>(3)  or "
"B<stpncpy>(3)."
msgstr ""

#.  ----- DESCRIPTION :: Length-bounded character sequences -----------/
#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:270
msgid ""
"To read a null-padded character sequence, use I<strnlen(src,\\ "
"countof(src))>, and then you can treat it as a length-bounded character "
"sequence; or use B<strncat>(3)  or B<strndup>(3)  directly."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:282
msgid ""
"The simplest character sequence copying function is B<mempcpy>(3).  It "
"requires always knowing the length of your character sequences, for which "
"structures can be used.  It makes the code much faster, since you always "
"know the length of your character sequences, and can do the minimal copies "
"and length measurements.  B<mempcpy>(3)  copies character sequences, so you "
"need to explicitly set the terminating null character if you need a string."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:289
msgid ""
"In programs that make considerable use of strings or character sequences, "
"and need the best performance, using overlapping character sequences can "
"make a big difference.  It allows holding subsequences of a larger character "
"sequence, while not duplicating memory nor using time to do a copy."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:295
msgid ""
"However, this is delicate, since it requires using character sequences.  C "
"library APIs use strings, so programs that use character sequences will have "
"to take care of differentiating strings from character sequences."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/string_copying.7:298
msgid "To copy a length-bounded character sequence, use B<mempcpy>(3)."
